<h2 id="apps-heading">Hinged Steel Belt Applications</h2>
<p><strong>CNC machine chip removal:</strong> The primary application. Hinged steel belts sit beneath CNC lathes, machining centers, and grinders to collect and convey metal chips from the cutting zone to a chip bin. The belt handles all chip morphologies: continuous turnings from lathes, broken chips from mills, and fine swarf from grinders. Mayfran, Jorgensen, LNS, and Hennig are leading chip conveyor manufacturers that integrate directly with major CNC machine brands.</p>
<p><strong>Stamping press scrap:</strong> Hinged steel belts remove blanking slugs, trim scrap, and skeleton scrap from stamping presses. The belt sits in a pit beneath the press or at floor level behind the press, receiving scrap as it falls from the die. Heavy-duty belts handle the impact loading of large slugs dropping from progressive and transfer dies. Multiple presses feed a common trunk-line hinged belt conveyor that delivers all scrap to a central collection point.</p>
<p><strong>Laser and plasma cutting:</strong> Hinged belts collect cut parts, slugs, and dross from laser cutting tables and plasma cutters. The belt handles hot dross particles and sharp cut edges without damage. Some configurations position the belt below the cutting table's slat support system to catch falling parts and scrap simultaneously.</p>
<p><strong>Die casting and foundry:</strong> Hinged steel belts transport hot die-cast parts, runners, gates, and flash from die-cast machines and trim presses. The belt withstands the 600-900°F temperatures of freshly cast aluminum and zinc parts. Coolant spray sections along the belt path cool parts during transport.</p>
<p><strong>Parts transfer:</strong> Beyond scrap, hinged steel belts transport finished parts between operations in metalworking plants. The rugged steel surface handles heavy, sharp, and oily parts that would damage rubber or plastic belts. Parts ride from one machine to the next for multi-operation processing.</p>
<p><strong>Coolant filtration systems:</strong> Hinged steel belts integrated with coolant tanks filter chips from cutting fluid. The belt lifts chips out of the coolant while fluid drains through the perforated belt back into the clean tank. This replaces standalone coolant filtration systems on machines with moderate chip volumes.</p>
<h2 id="sizing-heading">Sizing &amp; Selection</h2>
<h3>Belt Width</h3>
<p>Belt width must accommodate the chip discharge area of the machine tool. Standard widths: 6", 8", 10", 12", 15", 18", 24", 30", 36", and 48". For stamping press scrap, belt width must exceed the die width to catch all falling scrap. For CNC machines, the machine manufacturer's chip conveyor opening dimension determines the required belt width.</p>
<h3>Belt Speed</h3>
<p>Hinged steel belt speeds typically range from 10 to 30 FPM. Slower speeds (10-15 FPM) suit continuous chip removal where a steady trickle of chips falls onto the belt. Faster speeds (20-30 FPM) suit batch discharge from stamping presses where large volumes of scrap fall at once. Variable-speed drives allow the belt to run faster during heavy chip production and slower during light cuts.</p>
<h3>Discharge Height</h3>
<p>The discharge end of the belt inclines to dump material into a collection bin. Standard discharge heights range from 30" to 60" above floor level. Higher discharge requires a steeper incline angle, which may need magnetic assist for ferrous materials or <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated belt</a> features. Discharge height should clear the top of the collection bin plus 6-12 inches for material piling.</p>
<h3>Material Selection</h3>
<p>Carbon steel plates are standard and handle most metalworking applications. Stainless steel plates (304 or 316) serve applications with corrosive coolants, stainless steel machining, or food-adjacent manufacturing. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel conveyor guide</a> for alloy selection guidance. Hardened plates extend life in abrasive scrap applications but cost more.</p>
<h2 id="maint-heading">Maintenance Guide</h2>
<p><strong>Daily:</strong> Check for chip jams at the discharge point and hinge pin area. Remove stringy turnings that wrap around the head sprocket. Verify coolant drainage is unobstructed. Inspect the belt for obvious plate damage or missing plates.</p>
<p><strong>Weekly:</strong> Inspect hinge pins for excessive wear. Check belt tracking and alignment. Verify the take-up mechanism maintains proper tension. Clean the coolant return trough of sludge and fine chip accumulation.</p>
<p><strong>Monthly:</strong> Measure chain elongation to predict replacement timing. Lubricate the drive gearbox per manufacturer specifications. Inspect sprocket teeth for wear patterns that indicate misalignment. Check motor amperage against baseline to detect increasing friction or overloading.</p>
<p><strong>Belt replacement:</strong> Individual damaged plates can be replaced by removing the hinge pins on either side, sliding out the damaged plate, and inserting a replacement. This field-serviceable design is a major advantage over <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">wire mesh belts</a> that require full belt replacement when damaged. Full belt replacement every 5-15 years is typical depending on application severity and maintenance quality.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a hinged steel belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A hinged steel belt conveyor uses interlocking steel plates connected by hinge pins to form a rugged continuous belt. The overlapping plates handle hot chips, sharp scrap, and heavy metalworking debris that would destroy rubber or plastic belts. It is the standard chip removal system for CNC machines and stamping presses.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What can a hinged steel belt conveyor handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Metal chips, turnings, stamping scrap, die-cast parts, hot forgings, and heavy industrial waste. Handles temperatures up to 900°F, sharp edges, coolant exposure, and impact loading. Load capacity from light chip duty to over 1,000 lbs per linear foot for heavy stamping scrap.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How does a hinged steel belt differ from wire mesh?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Hinged steel belts use solid overlapping plates (closed surface) for heavy scrap and chip handling. <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">Wire mesh belts</a> use open-weave wire for airflow and drainage in ovens, freezers, and wash systems. Hinged steel handles heavier loads and sharper materials. Wire mesh handles heat transfer and drainage applications.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What maintenance does a hinged steel belt require?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Daily chip jam removal and drainage checks. Weekly hinge pin and plate inspection. Monthly chain tension and motor amperage checks. Individual damaged plates can be field-replaced without removing the entire belt. Typical belt life is 5-15 years depending on application severity.</p>

</div>
